WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
In the last video, you saw some of the wide range of applications to which you can apply sequence models.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.500
Let's start by defining a notation that we'll use to build up these sequence models.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.300
As a motivating example, let's say you want to build a sequence model to input a sentence like this.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.000
Harry Potter and Hermione Granger invented a new spell.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.300
And these are characters, by the way, from the Harry Potter sequence of novels by J.K. Rowling.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:34.300
And let's say you want a sequence model to automatically tell you where are the people's names in this sentence.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:40.200
So this is a problem called named entity recognition, and this is used by search engines, for example,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:46.300
to index all of the, say, the last 24 hours news of all the people mentioned in the news articles

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.000
so that they can index them appropriately.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
And name entity recognition systems can be used to find people's names, companies' names,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.500
times, locations, countries' names, currency names, and so on in different types of text.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:08.900
Now, given this input x, let's say that you want a model to output y that has one output per input word.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:16.200
And the target output, the desired y, tells you for each of the input words, is that part of a person's name.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.200
And technically, this maybe isn't the best output representation.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.600
There are some more sophisticated output representations that tells you not just,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.700
it's a word part of a person's name, but tells you where are the start and ends of people's names in the sentence.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.700
You might want to know Harry Potter starts here and ends here, starts here and ends here.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:41.200
But for this motivating example, I'm just going to stick with this simpler output representation.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.900
Now, the input is a sequence of nine words.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:50.400
So eventually, we're going to have nine sets of features to represent these nine words.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.900
And index into the positions in the sequence, I'm going to use x and then super strip angle brackets,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:05.900
1, 2, 3, and so on, up to x, angle bracket 9, to index into the different positions.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:13.900
I'm going to use x, t with an index t to index into positions in the middle of the sequence.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:20.400
And t implies that these are temporal sequences, although whether the sequence is a temporal one or not,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:26.400
I'm going to use the index t to index into the positions in the sequence.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:34.900
And similarly, for the output, we're going to refer to these outputs as y, angle bracket 1, 2, 3,

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.900
and so on, up to y, 9.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.400
Let's also use t subscript x to denote the length of the input sequence.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.400
So in this case, there are nine words.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:51.900
So t x is equal to 9 and we'll use t y to denote the length of the output sequence.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:58.400
In this example, t x is equal to t y, but it's on the last video, t x and t y can be different.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:05.400
So you will remember that in the notation we've been using, we've been writing x round brackets i

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.900
to denote the i-th training example.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:15.400
So to refer to the t-th element or the t-th element in the sequence of training example i,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.400
we'll use this notation.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:25.400
And if t x is the length of a sequence, then different examples in your training set can have different lengths.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:31.900
And so t x i will be the input sequence length for training example i.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:40.400
And similarly, y i t means the t-th element in the output sequence of the i-th training example,

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:48.400
and t y i will be the length of the output sequence in the i-th training example.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.400
So in this particular example, t x i is equal to 9, but if you had a different training example

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:02.400
with a sentence of 15 words, then t x i would be equal to 15 for that different training example.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:09.400
Now, this is our first series foray into NLP, or natural language processing.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:15.400
Let's next talk about how we would represent individual words in a sentence.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:21.400
So to represent the words in the sentence, the first thing you do is come up with a vocabulary,

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:30.400
sometimes also called a dictionary, and that means making a list of the words that you will use in your representation.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.400
So the first word in the vocabulary is a, that's the first word of the dictionary.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:40.400
The second word is a, aaron, and then a little bit further down is the word and,

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:48.400
and then eventually you get to the word harry, then eventually the word potter,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:57.160
and then all the way down to maybe the last word in the dictionary is a zulu.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:09.160
And so a would be word one, aaron is word two, and in my dictionary, the word and appears in position or index 367.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:23.110
Harry appears in position 4075, potter at position 6830, and zulu is the last word of the dictionary is maybe word 10,000.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:30.460
So in this example, I'm going to use a dictionary with size 10,000 words.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.460
This is quite small by modern NLP applications.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:40.460
For commercial applications, for model, for reasonable size commercial applications,

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:47.460
dictionary sizes of 30 to 50,000 are more common and 100,000 is not uncommon.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:54.460
And then some of the large internet companies will use dictionary sizes that are maybe a million words or even bigger than that.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:06:01.460
But you see a lot of commercial applications use dictionary sizes of maybe 30,000 or maybe 50,000 words.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:07.460
But I'm going to use 10,000 for illustration, since it's a nice round number.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:27.460
So if you have chosen a dictionary of 10,000 words, and one way to build this dictionary would be to look through your training sets and find the top 10,000 occurring words or to look through some of the online dictionaries that tells you what are the most common 10,000 words in the English language say.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:33.460
What you can do is then use one hot representations to represent each of these words.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:51.460
For example, X1, which represents the word Harry, would be a vector with all zeros except for a one in position 4075 because that was the position of Harry in the dictionary.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:07:03.460
And then X2 will be again, similarly a vector of all zeros except for one in position 6830 and then zeros everywhere else.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:08.460
The word and was represented as a position 367.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:17.460
So X3 would be a vector with zeros, a one in position 367 and then zeros everywhere else.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:25.460
And each of these would be a 10,000 dimensional vector if your vocabulary has 10,000 words.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:43.460
And this one, A, I guess, because A is the first word of the dictionary, then X7, which corresponds to the word A, that would be the vector one, you know, this is the first element of the dictionary and then zero everywhere else.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:56.460
So in this representation, X, T for each of the values of T in your sentence will be a one hot vector, one hot because there's exactly one one that's on and zeros everywhere else.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:01.460
And you would have nine of them to represent the nine words in this sentence.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:10.460
And the goal is given this representation for X to learn a mapping using a sequence model to the target output Y.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:17.460
And we'll do this as a supervised learning problem where we should give in label data with both X and Y.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:26.460
And just one last detail, which we'll talk more about in a later video is what if you encounter a word that is not in your vocabulary?

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:37.460
Well, the answer is you create a new token or a new fake word called unknown word, which I'm denote as follows, angle brackets, UNK to represent words not in your vocabulary.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.460
We'll come on to talk more about this later.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:48.460
So to summarize, in this video, we described a notation for describing your training set for both X and Y for sequence data.

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:55.460
In the next video, let's start to describe a recurrent neural network for learning the mapping from X to Y.
